<header><p class="st-kicker">city state · Greece and the Aegean</p><h1 class="st-title">Odessos</h1><p class="st-meta">585 BCE – 72 BCE (approx.) · capital Odessos</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Milesian colony on the Bay of Varna that linked Thracian kingdoms to Aegean trade for five centuries and grew into modern Varna.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Odessos was founded by colonists from <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/miletus">Miletus</a> on the western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-sea">Black Sea</a> coast, usually dated to c. 585-570 BCE, at the mouth of a valley leading inland towards <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thrace">Thrace</a>. The site had good anchorage and access to fertile land, and the colony grew through exchange with the Thracian <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/getae">Getae</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/odrysians">Odrysians</a> of the interior.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Odessos was a self-governing Greek polis whose prosperity rested on grain, fish, wine imports and trade with Thracian rulers, whose wealth appears in rich local tombs. Its chief deity was the Great God, a Hellenised form of the Thracian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/darzalas">Darzalas</a>, shown on its coins. It probably took part in the revolt of the West Pontic cities against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lysimachus">Lysimachus</a> in 313 BCE.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Like <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/tomis">Tomis</a>, <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/callatis">Callatis</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/mesembria">Mesembria</a>, Odessos came under Roman control after the campaign of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marcus-terentius-varro-lucullus">Marcus Terentius Varro Lucullus</a> in 72-71 BCE. As Odessus it prospered in the Roman province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moesia">Moesia</a>, with large public baths, and later passed to <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/byzantium">Byzantium</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgarians">Bulgarians</a>. The modern city is <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/varna">Varna</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="city state, 585 BCE – 72 BCE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M24 82 V42 H32 V34 H40 V42 H46 V34 H54 V42 H60 V34 H68 V42 H76 V82 Z"/><path d="M44 82 V66 Q50 58 56 66 V82"/><path d="M34 54 H40 M60 54 H66"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">city state</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Greece and the Aegean</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:48.06%;width:10.21%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>585 BCE – 72 BCE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/6th-century-bce">6th century BCE</a> – <a href="/centuries/1st-century-bce">1st century BCE</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>city state</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>mercantile</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Odessos</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Ancient Greek religion (cult of the Great God)</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Ancient Greek</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Odessus, Varna</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=-328&amp;focus=odessos" aria-label="Odessos on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5353 644 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Odessos lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5673 900L5304 942"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5304 942h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5673 900h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5673 900h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Odessos. <a href="/atlas?year=-328&amp;focus=odessos">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

